\section{Avoidance Source Diagnosis}\label{sec:avoidance_source}

Sections~\ref{sec:fake_safety} and~\ref{sec:ee_only} establish that standard CAR is contaminated by action collapse and that end-effector-only SDF refinement fails structurally. A constructive question remains: when collision avoidance does improve in fine-tuned models, where does the signal originate? We isolate the contribution of SDF geometric supervision from LoRA fine-tuning on collision-free demonstrations by comparing three conditions on Pi0.5: LoRA-only (no SDF), LoRA+SDF at $\lambda = 0.005$, and LoRA+SDF at $\lambda = 0.01$. All models are evaluated at the 30K-step checkpoint with rotation-corrected evaluation (Section~\ref{sec:dfcar}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Avoidance source attribution on SafeLIBERO (200 episodes per seed, 30K-step checkpoints). At $\lambda \in \{0.005, 0.01\}$, SDF adds no measurable CAR beyond LoRA-only ($p > 0.1$). All fine-tuned models exhibit 0\% frozen rate and displacement above 0.37\,m, confirming genuine active avoidance.}
\label{tab:avoidance_source}
\vspace{2pt}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Method & Seeds & TSR (\%) & CAR (\%) & EE Disp (m) & Frozen (\%) \\
\midrule
Pi0.5 baseline & 3 & 74.7\,$\pm$\,1.5 & 26.8\,$\pm$\,1.6 & 0.367 & 0.0 \\
\midrule
LoRA-only (no SDF) & 2 & 51.5 & 54.5\,$\pm$\,2.8 & 0.392 & 0.0 \\
LoRA+SDF $\lambda{=}0.005$ & 3 & 54.0\,$\pm$\,3.0 & 54.5\,$\pm$\,2.3 & 0.387 & 0.0 \\
LoRA+SDF $\lambda{=}0.01$ & 3 & 53.8\,$\pm$\,1.5 & 56.3\,$\pm$\,1.6 & 0.389 & 0.0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:avoidance_source} reports the results. LoRA fine-tuning without any SDF objective achieves CAR of 54.5\% across two seeds. LoRA+SDF at $\lambda = 0.005$ (three seeds) produces identical CAR of 54.5\%, and $\lambda = 0.01$ (three seeds) reaches 56.3\%. All fine-tuned configurations register 0\% frozen episodes and mean displacement above 0.37\,m, confirming that the collision avoidance reflected in CAR is genuine active motion. At $\lambda \in \{0.005, 0.01\}$, SDF does not improve CAR beyond the LoRA-only baseline ($p > 0.1$).

We construct a null distribution by pooling the two LoRA-only seeds and three $\lambda = 0.005$ seeds into five per-seed CAR measurements (mean 54.5\%, standard deviation 2.15\%), treating them as exchangeable because SDF at $\lambda = 0.005$ produces no separation from LoRA-only. The $\lambda = 0.01$ mean of 56.3\% falls within $0.5\sigma$ of this null ($p > 0.1$), consistent with seed-to-seed variation rather than a treatment effect.

A Phase~2 ablation reinforces this conclusion. Setting $\lambda = 0$ during Phase~2 (steps 5K--30K), so that SDF supervision is active only during auxiliary head warmup (Phase~1, steps 0--5K), yields CAR of 57.5\%. This value lies within the null range, indicating that SDF training produces no lasting behavioral effect on collision avoidance even when the model receives geometric gradients during early training.

The avoidance signal originates in the training distribution. The demonstration data consists exclusively of collision-free trajectories; a model that imitates these demonstrations reproduces obstacle-avoiding motion patterns from the training scenes. LoRA fine-tuning alone raises CAR from 26.8\% (Pi0.5 baseline) to ${\sim}$55\%, an improvement of ${\sim}$28 percentage points attributable entirely to collision-free demonstration data. SDF supervision at $\lambda \in \{0.005, 0.01\}$ is redundant with the avoidance information already encoded in these trajectories.

\subsection{CAR--TSR Trade-off}\label{sec:tsr_collapse}

All fine-tuned models exhibit moderate TSR degradation relative to the Pi0.5 baseline: 74.7\% $\to$ 51.5--54.0\% at 30K steps, a reduction of ${\sim}$21--23 percentage points. The SDF loss is not responsible for this degradation. LoRA-only drops by 23.2 points (to 51.5\%), while SDF variants drop by 20.7--20.9 points (to 53.8--54.0\%). SDF-augmented models show TSR 2.3--2.5 percentage points above LoRA-only, within seed-to-seed noise. Table~\ref{tab:per_task_tsr} (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_tsr_pertask}) decomposes TSR by task. The degradation is non-uniform: Chocolate Pudding retains most of its pre-training capability (${\sim}$68--78\% across conditions), while Orange Juice degrades most (${\sim}$25--33\%). Milk and BBQ Sauce occupy intermediate positions (35--49\% and 59--69\%, respectively). No task collapses to near-zero; the per-task pattern is similar between SDF and LoRA-only conditions, confirming that the SDF loss term does not drive the degradation.

The TSR reduction reflects a distributional shift in the policy, not learned paralysis. All fine-tuned models retain substantial EE motion (mean displacement ${\sim}$0.39\,m, 0\% frozen episodes): the robot continues to move actively through the workspace. The simultaneous CAR improvement and TSR degradation arise from the same source. The demonstration data consists exclusively of collision-free trajectories; LoRA fine-tuning on this data teaches obstacle-avoiding motion patterns, raising CAR by ${\sim}$28 percentage points. The same distributional shift moves the policy away from the pre-trained manipulation prior, reducing task success by ${\sim}$21--23 percentage points. The non-uniform per-task pattern---Chocolate Pudding retains ${\sim}$68--78\% while Orange Juice drops to ${\sim}$25--33\%---suggests that vulnerability to fine-tuning varies by task, potentially reflecting differences in grasp geometry or coverage in the demonstration distribution.

\subsection{Dose-Response at Higher Loss Weights}\label{sec:lambda_high}

\placeholder{Pending: $\lambda = 0.1$ results (expected 2026-05-27).} The experiments above establish that SDF supervision at $\lambda \in \{0.005, 0.01\}$ is redundant with collision-free demonstrations. Whether higher loss weights produce a dose-dependent CAR improvement remains an open question. Three outcomes are possible:

\begin{itemize}
\item[(a)] If $\lambda = 0.1$ achieves CAR above 65\%, a dose-dependent relationship exists between SDF loss weight and collision avoidance, indicating that sufficient geometric gradient signal can exceed the avoidance provided by demonstrations alone.
\item[(b)] If CAR falls in the 60--65\% range, a weaker dose-response is present but the practical gain over demonstrations alone is modest.
\item[(c)] If CAR remains at or below 57\%, SDF supervision is fully redundant across the tested $\lambda$ range, and collision-free demonstrations account for all observed avoidance behavior.
\end{itemize}

The final narrative for this section will be updated once the $\lambda = 0.1$ evaluation completes.

